<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- v9.15.0 "Private Fetch": no cleartext anywhere. Every online lookup
     must be https, so a query is never sent in the clear.

     v9.33.42 "Borrowed brain": ONE exception, and it is a real trade. A model
     server on the user's own Wi-Fi speaks plain http, so a LAN address cannot
     be reached at all while this is false. Android's config cannot scope
     cleartext to private address ranges - it takes hostnames - so the choice
     is either this, or the feature cannot exist.

     What it costs: the app itself would now be ALLOWED to make a cleartext
     call. It does not - the only cleartext connection in the codebase is the
     one to the address the user typed on their own network (RemoteEngine).
     Every other lookup still goes through the https-only helper in
     OnlineFetch. -->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
